  "Number": 22,
  "Question": "The number \\(121_b,\\) written in the integral base \\(b,\\) is the square of an integer, for:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(b=10,\\) only",
    "B": "\\(b=10\\) and \\(b=5,\\) only",
    "C": "\\(2\\le b\\le10\\)",
    "D": "\\(b\\gt2\\)",
    "E": "no value of \\(b\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "In base \\(b\\), the three positions represent multiples of \\(b^2\\), \\(b\\), and \\(1\\). Thus\n\\[\n121_b=b^2+2b+1=(b+1)^2.\n\\]\nSince \\(b\\) is an integer, \\(b+1\\) is an integer, so this is an integer square whenever the numeral is valid. The permitted digits in a standard integral base are \\(0,1,\\ldots,b-1\\); therefore the digit \\(2\\) requires \\(b>2\\). In particular, \\(121_2\\) is not a valid base-two numeral. The answer is every integral base \\(b>2\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
